Edition No. 42 — Thursday, 12 November 2026

10 stories: A flash disk, five paragraphs and a second objection: the treason bail hearing stalls again, and the court rules on Friday whether the raid video stays on the record (lead); Ordered to court at nine, not produced: the State says Jay Jay Banda's doctor will not let him leave Maina Soko, and the judge demands a medical report before Friday; US$30.89 billion owed, US$275 million saved: external debt fell 5% after the Eurobond buyback, Musokotwane tells Parliament, as the IMF board weighs a US$1.46 billion programme; K12,253.72 to keep a Lusaka family of five: the JCTR basket jumps K854 in a month, and the centre says the budget should have taxed copper windfalls, not wage earners; 'A true leader should never take people for granted': from his Choma farm, Hichilema tells MPs who wanted ministries to be grateful for their seats and the party to stay united; 'Verification and recruitment can run together': Tonse says freezing teacher hiring in 2027 contradicts what the UPND once demanded of the PF; the Treasury says count first; Planning for a dry season: ZESCO maps 11,630MW by 2030 with hydro down to a third, Kapala tells the REA to connect paid-up customers, and Nkana Water warns Kitwe of rationing; One goal to find on Sunday: Nouhoun Koné's 21st-minute strike gives Burkina Faso a 1–0 first-leg lead over Zambia's under-23s in Ouagadougou after a delayed flight; Friday the 13th in Ndola: Zambia, second in Group I on three points, meet bottom-placed Burundi without Daka, Banda and Lungu, with the return in Bujumbura four days later; 'Happy heavenly 70th birthday, Dad': Tasila Lungu marks the day her father would have turned seventy, seventeen months after his death and with his remains still in South Africa.

